<!-- ============= PREMISE ============= -->
<div class="levp-band levp-premise">
  <div class="levp-inner">
    <div class="levp-premise-grid">
      <div class="levp-premise-thesis">
        <div class="levp-eyebrow">The premise</div>
        <h2 class="levp-h2">A habit isn't only chemical. <span class="levp-italic">It's also a ritual.</span></h2>
        <p class="levp-lede">
          Decades of cessation research show that the behavioral and sensorimotor components of a habit — the hand-to-mouth motion, the breath drawn in, the throat sensation, the scent — drive cravings independently of any active drug. Lio is engineered to satisfy that layer.
        </p>
        <div class="levp-premise-pull">
          <p>"Sniffing either a pleasant or unpleasant odor reduced reported urge to smoke relative to the control odor."</p>
          <cite>Sayette &amp; Parrott, 1999 · Experimental and Clinical Psychopharmacology <span class="levp-ref" data-ref="3">3</span></cite>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="levp-premise-points">
        <div class="levp-premise-pt">
          <div class="levp-premise-num"><strong>01</strong>Behavioral science</div>
          <div>
            <h3>The ritual is roughly half the habit.</h3>
            <p>A 1994 randomized study at Duke gave 48 nicotine-deprived smokers either a black-pepper vapor inhaler, a mint/menthol inhaler, or an empty cartridge. The black-pepper group reported significantly reduced craving and withdrawal versus the empty control — without any nicotine being delivered. The conclusion: a substantial part of withdrawal is sensory, not chemical. <span class="levp-ref" data-ref="1">1</span></p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="levp-premise-pt">
          <div class="levp-premise-num"><strong>02</strong>Olfactory cues</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Pleasant scents reduce peak urge.</h3>
            <p>In a 2019 follow-up replication (n = 232), researchers at Pittsburgh and Brown confirmed that a pleasant olfactory cue, sniffed at peak craving, produced a measurable drop in self-reported urge — sustained for several minutes, not seconds. <span class="levp-ref" data-ref="2">2</span> The original 1999 paper reported the urge drop as roughly double that of a control. <span class="levp-ref" data-ref="3">3</span></p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="levp-premise-pt">
          <div class="levp-premise-num"><strong>03</strong>Substitution</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Sensorimotor replacement is itself a treatment category.</h3>
            <p>The UK National Health Service has trialed de-nicotinised cigarettes specifically because the act, the breath, and the throat sensation matter <em>independently</em> of nicotine. <span class="levp-ref" data-ref="4">4</span> Lio applies the same principle without combustion, vapor, or any active drug.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="levp-premise-pt">
          <div class="levp-premise-num"><strong>04</strong>Mechanism</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Aromatherapy shows a small but consistent effect on cravings.</h3>
            <p>A 2013 randomized study published in the <em>Journal of Alternative and Complementary Medicine</em> (n=20) and a 2022 controlled pilot (n=58) both reported reduced nicotine craving following short olfactory exposure to black-pepper or angelica essential oil. <span class="levp-ref" data-ref="5">5</span><span class="levp-ref" data-ref="6">6</span> Effect sizes are modest. Effects are real.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ============= INGREDIENT DOSSIER ============= -->
<div class="levp-band">
  <div class="levp-inner">
    <div class="levp-dossier-head">
      <div class="levp-eyebrow">Ingredient dossier</div>
      <h2 class="levp-h2">Three plants. <span class="levp-italic">All studied. All cited.</span></h2>
      <p class="levp-lede">Lio cores are infused with essential oils sourced from IFRA-certified EU farms. Each ingredient was selected because it has independently published research on inhalation and craving — not because of how it tastes.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="levp-dossier-grid">
      <article class="levp-dossier-card">
        <div class="levp-dossier-num">INGREDIENT &nbsp;·&nbsp; 01 / 03</div>
        <div class="levp-dossier-latin">Mentha × piperita</div>
        <div class="levp-dossier-name">Peppermint</div>
        <div class="levp-dossier-chips">
          <span class="levp-dossier-chip">Cooling</span>
          <span class="levp-dossier-chip">Menthol</span>
          <span class="levp-dossier-chip">Throat sensation</span>
          <span class="levp-dossier-chip">Alertness</span>
        </div>
        <p class="levp-dossier-find">
          Peppermint is the most-studied aroma compound in our blend. <strong>Hirsch's 2008 study</strong> at the Smell &amp; Taste Treatment Foundation found that participants who inhaled peppermint scent every two hours consumed roughly 2,700 fewer calories over the test week and reported significantly lower hunger ratings. <strong>Kennedy et al. (2018)</strong>, published in <em>Nutrients</em>, reported improved cognitive performance and reduced mental fatigue at higher peppermint doses. The cooling sensation also closely mimics the menthol throat-hit familiar to most former vape users.
        </p>
        <ul class="levp-dossier-cites">
          <li><span class="levp-ref" data-ref="7">7</span> Hirsch, A. R. (2008). <em>Effects of peppermint scent on appetite control and caloric intake.</em></li>
          <li><span class="levp-ref" data-ref="8">8</span> Kennedy, D. O., et al. (2018). <em>Volatile terpenes and brain function</em>, Nutrients, 10(8).</li>
          <li><span class="levp-ref" data-ref="9">9</span> Kazemi, A., et al. (2024). <em>Peppermint and menthol: review</em>, Crit. Rev. Food Sci. Nutr.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="levp-dossier-card">
        <div class="levp-dossier-num">INGREDIENT &nbsp;·&nbsp; 02 / 03</div>
        <div class="levp-dossier-latin">Piper nigrum</div>
        <div class="levp-dossier-name">Black Pepper</div>
        <div class="levp-dossier-chips">
          <span class="levp-dossier-chip">Warming</span>
          <span class="levp-dossier-chip">Earthy</span>
          <span class="levp-dossier-chip">Throat hit</span>
          <span class="levp-dossier-chip">Most-cited</span>
        </div>
        <p class="levp-dossier-find">
          The most directly relevant ingredient to Lio's category. <strong>Rose &amp; Behm (1994)</strong> at Duke ran the foundational randomized study: 48 abstinent smokers, three conditions (black pepper / mint-menthol / empty), with the black-pepper group showing significant reductions in craving and withdrawal symptoms versus control. <strong>Cordell &amp; Buckle (2013)</strong> extended this to a US-campus cohort and reported a similar effect. <strong>Yılmaz &amp; Karadağ (2022)</strong> confirmed the finding in a randomized pilot (n=58).
        </p>
        <ul class="levp-dossier-cites">
          <li><span class="levp-ref" data-ref="1">1</span> Rose, J. E., &amp; Behm, F. M. (1994). <em>Drug Alcohol Depend.</em>, 34(3), 225–229.</li>
          <li><span class="levp-ref" data-ref="5">5</span> Cordell, B., &amp; Buckle, J. (2013). <em>J. Altern. Complement. Med.</em>, 19(8), 709–713.</li>
          <li><span class="levp-ref" data-ref="6">6</span> Yılmaz, D., &amp; Karadağ, A. (2022). Randomized controlled pilot trial on nicotine craving.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="levp-dossier-card">
        <div class="levp-dossier-num">INGREDIENT &nbsp;·&nbsp; 03 / 03</div>
        <div class="levp-dossier-latin">Eucalyptus globulus</div>
        <div class="levp-dossier-name">Eucalyptus</div>
        <div class="levp-dossier-chips">
          <span class="levp-dossier-chip">Crisp</span>
          <span class="levp-dossier-chip">Respiratory</span>
          <span class="levp-dossier-chip">1,8-cineole</span>
          <span class="levp-dossier-chip">Calming</span>
        </div>
        <p class="levp-dossier-find">
          Eucalyptus oil is dominated by <strong>1,8-cineole</strong>, an active compound studied for over forty years for its respiratory and cognitive effects. A 2017 review in <em>Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine</em> summarized its inhalation safety profile and documented its anti-inflammatory and mucolytic actions in human trials at therapeutic doses. In Lio it serves a sensory role — the cool, open feeling on the inhale — at concentrations far below therapeutic levels.
        </p>
        <ul class="levp-dossier-cites">
          <li><span class="levp-ref" data-ref="10">10</span> Sadlon &amp; Lamson (2010). <em>Immune-modifying and antimicrobial effects of Eucalyptus oil and simple inhalation devices.</em></li>
          <li><span class="levp-ref" data-ref="11">11</span> Caldas, G. F. R., et al. (2015). Therapeutic profile of <em>Eucalyptus globulus</em> essential oil.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

