<!-- ============= SAFETY (vape vs Lio) ============= -->
<div class="levp-safety">
  <div class="levp-inner">
    <div class="levp-safety-head">
      <div class="levp-eyebrow">Mechanism &amp; safety</div>
      <h2 class="levp-h2">No heat. No vapor. <span class="levp-italic">No combustion.</span></h2>
      <p class="levp-lede">Most safety questions about Lio come from people who have read about vape toxicology. Those concerns apply to a different category of device. Here is what each one actually does.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="levp-safety-grid">
      <div class="levp-safety-col is-vape">
        <div class="levp-safety-label"><span class="levp-marker"></span> A vape</div>
        <h3>Aerosolizes a heated liquid</h3>
        <ul class="levp-safety-flow">
          <li class="muted"><span class="levp-step">1</span> Battery powers a heating coil</li>
          <li class="muted"><span class="levp-step">2</span> Coil heats e-liquid to ~200–300°C</li>
          <li class="muted"><span class="levp-step">3</span> Liquid aerosolizes into vapor</li>
          <li class="muted"><span class="levp-step">4</span> User inhales aerosol particles</li>
          <li class="muted"><span class="levp-step">5</span> Vapor exits the user's lungs</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="levp-safety-out"><strong>What enters the lungs:</strong> aerosolized propylene glycol, glycerin, nicotine (in most), flavorant compounds heated past their ingestion-safety profile. <span class="levp-ref" data-ref="13">13</span><span class="levp-ref" data-ref="14">14</span></p>
      </div>

      <div class="levp-safety-col is-lio">
        <div class="levp-safety-label"><span class="levp-marker"></span> Lio</div>
        <h3>Passes air across a fiber matrix</h3>
        <ul class="levp-safety-flow">
          <li class="lit"><span class="levp-step">1</span> User inhales through the wooden barrel</li>
          <li class="lit"><span class="levp-step">2</span> Air flows across an oil-infused core</li>
          <li class="lit"><span class="levp-step">3</span> Air picks up plant aromatic molecules</li>
          <li class="lit"><span class="levp-step">4</span> Scent travels on the user's own breath</li>
          <li class="lit"><span class="levp-step">5</span> Nothing exits the user's mouth</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="levp-safety-out"><strong>What enters the lungs:</strong> ambient air carrying volatile aromatic compounds — the same molecules diffused in any aromatherapy room. No combustion. No aerosolization. No exhaled cloud.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="levp-safety-note">
      <strong>One honest caveat.</strong> The long-term safety of essential-oil inhalation in any context — Lio, a diffuser, a room spray — is not as exhaustively studied as inhalation-pharmaceutical compounds. The compounds in Lio's cores have a long history of safe use in aromatherapy and food applications, but if you have asthma, COPD, or any respiratory condition, talk with your doctor before starting. We would rather lose the sale than cause an issue.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ============= LIMITATIONS ============= -->
<div class="levp-limits">
  <div class="levp-limits-inner">
    <div class="levp-eyebrow">What the evidence does not say</div>
    <h2 class="levp-h2">Things <span class="levp-italic">we will not claim.</span></h2>
    <p class="levp-lede">A healthy skepticism is the right posture toward any wellness product. The page above tells you what the literature does support. This section is the equally important other half — what it does not.</p>

    <ul class="levp-limits-list">
      <li>
        <span class="levp-limits-tag">— Limit 01</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Lio is not a smoking-cessation drug.</h3>
          <p>It has not been submitted to or approved by the FDA, EMA, or any equivalent body as a cessation aid. The nicotine cravings literature cited above describes ingredient effects, not a regulatory clearance for the Lio device itself. Lio is registered and marketed in the EU as a wellness and aromatherapy device.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="levp-limits-tag">— Limit 02</span>
        <div>
          <h3>The studies we cite are small.</h3>
          <p>The foundational Rose &amp; Behm trial had 48 participants. The 2013 Cordell &amp; Buckle study had 20. These are pilot-scale, not phase-3. The effect direction is consistent across thirty years of independent replications, but the absolute effect sizes are modest.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="levp-limits-tag">— Limit 03</span>
        <div>
          <h3>It will not work for everyone.</h3>
          <p>Habits run on many circuits — sensory, social, emotional, chemical. Lio addresses the sensorimotor-and-scent layer well. Heavy chemical dependence, severe psychological distress, or social-context triggers may need professional support. Our 30-day return policy exists for exactly this reason: if Lio doesn't fit your specific habit shape, send it back.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="levp-limits-tag">— Limit 04</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Long-term inhalation data is still incomplete.</h3>
          <p>Decades of data exist for short-term aromatherapy use; long-term, multi-year inhalation studies of any aromatic compound are scarcer. The compounds Lio uses are food-safe and IFRA-certified for fragrance use, but we will be candid: nobody has run a 20-year cohort study specifically on a product like this. We monitor the literature and will update this page when better data exists.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="levp-limits-tag">— Limit 05</span>
        <div>
          <h3>We are the manufacturer. We have an interest.</h3>
          <p>Everything on this page is sourced. The interpretation is ours. If you want a fully independent perspective, every reference below is publicly searchable, and we encourage you to read the originals before forming a view.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

